---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../components/SiteFooter.astro';
import { siteConfig } from '../config/site';
import { withBase } from '../lib/site-path';

const snippets = {
  inline: String.raw`A state is $|\psi\rangle$.
Or write \(e^{i\pi}+1=0\).`,
  display: String.raw`$$
i\hbar\frac{\partial\psi}{\partial t}
=\hat H\psi
$$

\[
\int_0^1 x^2\,dx=\frac13
\]`,
  hackmd: String.raw`$$
ds_{\mathrm{ind}}^2 =
-\sin^2\theta_0\,dt^2
+4L^2\sin^2\theta_0\,d\Omega_5^2.
$$`,
  aligned: String.raw`$$
\begin{aligned}
(a+b)^2 &= a^2+2ab+b^2 \\
        &= (a-b)^2+4ab
\end{aligned}
\tag{1}
$$`,
  structure: '# A clear title\n\n## A useful section\n\n**Bold**, *italic*, and [a link](https://qft.org).\n\n> A thought worth keeping.\n\n1. Start with the idea.\n2. Follow the calculation.',
  table: '| Quantity | Value |\n| :--- | ---: |\n| $n$ | 3 |\n| $E_n$ | $\\hbar\\omega(n+1/2)$ |',
  code: '```python\nimport math\n\ndef gaussian(x):\n    return math.exp(-x * x)\n```',
  notes: 'A useful observation.[^note]\n\n[^note]: Add the detail here.\n\n- [x] Check the assumptions\n- [ ] Finish the derivation',
  macros: String.raw`$\gdef\RR{\mathbb R}$

Let $x\in\RR$ and $v\in\RR^n$.`,
  image: '![A description of the figure](https://example.org/figure.png)',
  interactive: '## Watch a wave packet spread\n\n```interactive wave-packet\n```',
  customDemo: [
    '```interactive title="A small counter"',
    '<button id="counter">Count: 0</button>',
    '<script>',
    '  let count = 0;',
    '  const button = document.getElementById("counter");',
    '  button.addEventListener("click", () => {',
    '    button.textContent = `Count: ${++count}`;',
    '  });',
    '</script>',
    '```',
  ].join('\n'),
  slides: String.raw`# A short presentation

One idea at a time.

---

## Time evolution

\[
i\hbar\,\partial_t|\psi\rangle=\hat H|\psi\rangle
\]

---

## A useful conclusion

- State the assumption.
- Explain the result.`,
};
---
<BaseLayout title={`Markdown, interactive slides & PDF guide — ${siteConfig.name}`} active="guide" description="Create AI-assisted teaching slides, revise portable lesson projects and interactive demos, check compatibility, and export Markdown, PDF, or standalone HTML.">
  <main id="main-content" class="content-page">
    <span class="page-kicker">From a useful answer to a lasting note</span>
    <h1>A guide to Markdown, math, and slides.</h1>
    <p class="page-lead">Use {siteConfig.name} to turn plain text or Markdown into documents and presentations with equations, pictures, and interactive demonstrations. Edit locally, present fullscreen, and keep portable copies of your work.</p>

    <section aria-labelledby="paste-to-document">
      <h2 id="paste-to-document">From pasted text to PDF</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Bring your text.</strong> Choose <strong>Paste</strong>, then add plain text or Markdown to a new document or the current one. An optional title becomes the new document name or an appended section heading. For equations and code, copy their Markdown or TeX source when it is available.</li>
        <li><strong>Review the formatting.</strong> Open <strong>Clean up</strong> and choose <strong>General Markdown</strong>. It can remove an outer Markdown wrapper, raise heading levels together, and trim outer blank lines. Review the highlighted changes, then choose <strong>Copy Markdown</strong> or <strong>Create formatted copy</strong>. Your original stays in the document list; editor Undo is available too.</li>
        <li><strong>Arrange your sections.</strong> <strong>Organize</strong> moves whole sections with their paragraphs, smaller headings, code, and equations. A document title and its introduction stay in place. Use <strong>Undo move</strong> to reverse a move.</li>
        <li><strong>Choose a presentation.</strong> Open <strong>Save PDF</strong> and choose Clean, Study Notes, or Academic. Adjust paper, margins, and text size, then save through the browser’s print dialog. <strong>Download HTML</strong> uses the same style.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>The typography preview shows your chosen appearance; the browser’s print preview shows final page boundaries. Document details include an optional author, a table of contents, and page breaks before major sections. <strong>Add a title if missing</strong> preserves the headings in your source.</p>
      <p>Find a saved note with the document search. Presentation settings stay with that draft in this browser and do not change the downloaded Markdown. Cleanup adjusts formatting; it leaves wording and source links as supplied.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="hackmd-formatting">
      <h2 id="hackmd-formatting">Prepare Markdown for HackMD</h2>
      <p>Open <strong>Clean up</strong>, choose <strong>HackMD</strong> under <strong>Format for</strong>, and review the suggested changes. <strong>Prepare mathematics for HackMD</strong> converts recognized backslash math delimiters to dollar delimiters and repairs mathematical lines that could be interpreted as Markdown structure when it can do so safely.</p>
      <p>A line containing only <code>=</code> can become a heading underline even between <code>$$</code> delimiters. Keeping the equals sign with the left-hand side avoids that conflict:</p>
      <pre><code>{snippets.hackmd}</code></pre>
      <p>A mathematical line starting with <code>&gt;</code>, such as <code>&gt;0.</code>, can become a Markdown blockquote. The formatter joins it to the preceding math line when safe, preserving the inequality. Lines after TeX comments and expressions with macro definitions remain unchanged for review.</p>
      <p><strong>Remove link tracking</strong> is selected by default when links contain UTM tags or common tracking IDs, such as <code>?utm_source=chatgpt.com</code>. Review the cleaned links before copying. Other URL parameters, anchors, and link text are kept; signed links remain unchanged. Uncheck the option when you want to keep the original URLs.</p>
      <p>Choose <strong>Copy for HackMD</strong>, then paste into HackMD's editor and check its preview. Choose <strong>Create formatted copy</strong> when you want to keep editing the result here. Copying remains available when the checks find no changes; creating a new formatted draft requires selected changes. If clipboard access fails, select and copy the visible result.</p>
      <p>Review any compatibility notes. The formatter preserves code examples and genuine mathematical row breaks, and leaves ambiguous escaping for review. Custom macros may render differently in HackMD's MathJax configuration. Copying source does not upload local pictures or transfer interactive demonstrations; upload the pictures there or use accessible image URLs, and use an HTML export for working demos.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="ai-lessons">
      <h2 id="ai-lessons">From an AI brief to a classroom lesson</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Describe your class.</strong> Choose <strong>Create with AI</strong> and enter the topic, audience, language, duration, mathematical depth, and demonstrations. Copy the instructions into your AI tool. Download the starter ZIP if you want to give it a working example.</li>
        <li><strong>Bring the complete lesson.</strong> Give your AI the chapter or source material there, and ask it for the project ZIP described in the instructions. Open that ZIP with <strong>Open AI lesson</strong>, <strong>Open file</strong>, or drag and drop. Your text and files are processed locally in the browser.</li>
        <li><strong>Revise and check.</strong> Edit slide text directly. Choose <strong>Edit demo</strong> to change a built-in demonstration's starting values or a custom demo's HTML. Use <strong>Lesson files</strong> for other source files. <strong>Check lesson</strong> reports compatibility problems; copy its report to AI for a revision.</li>
        <li><strong>Rehearse and keep a copy.</strong> Run the demos, choose <strong>Present</strong>, and check the explanation and slide fit. Download <strong>Project ZIP</strong> for editing and <strong>HTML</strong> for a classroom copy that opens independently. PDF is a static handout.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>When AI revises a lesson, give it the latest Project ZIP and ask it to preserve the project identity and paths. Reopen its ZIP to review the changes, then update the saved lesson or import a new copy. An update retains the earlier version as a recovery draft. A compatibility check does not establish scientific correctness or test JavaScript behavior; rehearse the actual presentation before class.</p>
      <p>AI authors and developers can use the <a href={withBase('lesson-format-v2.md')}>version-2 lesson format guide</a> and <a href={withBase('lesson.schema.json')}>manifest JSON schema</a>. These describe ordinary Markdown, local pictures, and separate self-contained demo files. The site does not call an AI model or require an API key.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="insert-images">
      <h2 id="insert-images">Add pictures and keep them with your work</h2>
      <p>Choose <strong>Insert image</strong>, paste an image into the editor, or drop image files onto the workspace. PNG, JPEG, WebP, and GIF are supported, up to <strong>8 MiB per image</strong>, <strong>24 MiB in total</strong>, and <strong>50 images per document</strong>. Imported pictures stay with the draft in this browser; they are not uploaded.</p>
      <p>Choose <strong>Markdown + images</strong> in Download to save an editable ZIP bundle. It includes the Markdown, local pictures, and slide settings. For a lesson project, this option is called <strong>Project ZIP</strong> and also keeps its demo and source files. Use <strong>Open file</strong> to reopen that ZIP here or in another browser. A plain Markdown download contains text and image references, so keep the bundle when your work includes imported pictures.</p>
      <p>You can also link to an image on the web using Markdown:</p>
      <pre><code>{snippets.image}</code></pre>
      <p>Web images stay blocked until you enable <strong>Load remote images</strong> in Settings. Enabling them contacts the image host. Portable HTML embeds enabled remote images only when the host allows the required download. A Markdown file opened by itself cannot read neighboring image files; use an exported bundle to move local pictures with a draft.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="markdown-slides">
      <h2 id="markdown-slides">Make a presentation from Markdown</h2>
      <p>Choose <strong>Slides</strong> in the Document / Slides selector. The Editor / Split / Preview controls still choose which panes you see. Separate slides with <code>---</code> on its own line, with a blank line before and after it. Separators inside fenced code or displayed mathematics stay literal. In Document mode, a separator is an ordinary horizontal rule.</p>
      <pre><code>{snippets.slides}</code></pre>
      <p>Slide settings offer <strong>Paper</strong>, <strong>Midnight</strong>, and <strong>Academic</strong> themes, with <strong>16:9</strong> or <strong>4:3</strong> proportions. Equations use the same four delimiters as documents: <code>$...$</code>, <code>$$...$$</code>, <code>{String.raw`\(...\)`}</code>, and <code>{String.raw`\[...\]`}</code>.</p>
      <p>Choose <strong>Present</strong> for fullscreen playback, use the arrow keys to move between slides, and press <kbd>Esc</kbd> to leave fullscreen. Keep each slide focused; split dense text or a long derivation across several slides when the content does not fit.</p>
      <p>In Slides mode, <strong>Save PDF</strong> prepares one page per slide through the browser’s print dialog. Check the print preview; Chromium-based browsers are recommended for consistent slide output. <strong>Download HTML</strong> creates a standalone interactive presentation with its fonts, images, and playback code included. Download <strong>Slide bundle</strong> to retain editable source and slide settings, or <strong>Markdown + images</strong> when the draft has local pictures. Both are ZIP bundles you can reopen here.</p>
      <p>This is a simple Markdown slide format, powered by reveal.js. Marp directives and Slidev components are not supported. Ordinary HTML and JavaScript remain readable source; the explicit interactive blocks below provide runnable demonstrations. Start with the <a href={withBase('?example=quantum-slides')}>five-slide quantum phase example</a>.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="interactive-demonstrations">
      <h2 id="interactive-demonstrations">Teach with an interactive demonstration</h2>
      <p>Choose <strong>Interactive demo</strong> to add a ready-made physics demonstration or your own HTML, CSS, and JavaScript. Keep <strong>Add on a new slide</strong> selected to give it a slide of its own. The demonstration appears as a static preview until you choose <strong>Run demo</strong>. Its sliders, buttons, and canvas work inside the slide, including in fullscreen. <strong>Stop</strong> removes the running demonstration; <strong>Restart</strong> returns it to its initial state.</p>
      <p>Timers and animations pause while a slide or browser tab is inactive and resume when you return. Choose <strong>Edit demo</strong> to save a built-in demo's starting values or revise custom HTML. Editing the Markdown recreates the rendered demo; temporary slider positions and animation time during playback are not saved.</p>
      <p>The four built-in demonstrations are a spreading Gaussian wave packet, a rotatable Bloch sphere, a Schwarzschild scalar-field potential, and wave interference. Try the <a href={withBase('?example=interactive-physics')}>interactive physics lesson</a> to present them together.</p>
      <pre><code>{snippets.interactive}</code></pre>
      <p>Replace <code>wave-packet</code> with <code>bloch-sphere</code>, <code>effective-potential</code>, or <code>interference</code>. Put one demonstration on a slide with only a short heading. Put the prediction question and explanation on the preceding slide so the plot and its controls have room.</p>
      <p>An empty built-in fence uses default parameters. A JSON object in the body defines saved initial values; <strong>Edit demo</strong> writes this for you. For example, a wave packet can use <code>{'{"sigma":1.2,"momentum":0.5,"time":0,"view":"density"}'}</code>. The values are restored when you run or restart the demonstration and are included in exports.</p>
      <h3>Bring your own HTML and JavaScript</h3>
      <p>Use an <code>interactive</code> fence with a title and self-contained HTML. Styles and scripts belong inside the block. Attach events with <code>addEventListener</code>; ordinary <code>html</code> and <code>javascript</code> fences still display code without running it.</p>
      <pre><code>{snippets.customDemo}</code></pre>
      <p>A lesson project can instead keep the source in a separate file. Use an empty fence with <code>interactive src="demos/explore.html" title="Explore the model"</code>. Choose <strong>Edit demo</strong> on its preview to revise that file, or open it through <strong>Lesson files</strong>. Download <strong>Project ZIP</strong> to keep the Markdown and demo files together. A plain Markdown download cannot include a separately linked demo.</p>
      <p>Each running demonstration uses a separate sandboxed frame. It cannot read the editor’s drafts or change its interface. External scripts, module imports, network requests, embedded frames, and inline event attributes such as <code>onclick</code> are not supported. Include the needed code and styles directly, and use embedded raster data images for assets. The editor does not convert a React or Slidev project into a standalone demo.</p>
      <p>A document can contain up to <strong>20 demonstrations</strong>, with up to <strong>100,000 characters</strong> of source in each custom demo. The complete Markdown still has the editor’s 400,000-character preview limit.</p>
      <h3>Share the demonstration and print its explanation</h3>
      <p><strong>Download HTML</strong> includes the demo code and player, so the built-in demonstrations and supported self-contained custom demos can run offline. Recipients choose <strong>Run demo</strong> themselves. Markdown and ZIP bundles retain the demonstration’s source, rather than its current slider positions or animation time.</p>
      <p><strong>Save PDF</strong> uses the most recently captured canvas image when available. Otherwise it uses the built-in demonstration’s initial illustration, or a labeled static placeholder for a custom demo. PDF pages do not run JavaScript. For a custom demonstration without a canvas, add an ordinary explanatory image to the Markdown when you need a specific figure in the PDF.</p>
    </section>

    <div class="guide-grid">
      <section class="guide-card">
        <h2>Math within a sentence</h2>
        <p>Use <code>$...$</code> or <code>{String.raw`\(...\)`}</code> for inline mathematics. Keep the formula next to its opening and closing dollar signs.</p>
        <pre><code>{snippets.inline}</code></pre>
      </section>
      <section class="guide-card">
        <h2>Give an equation space</h2>
        <p>Put <code>$$</code> or <code>{String.raw`\[`}</code> and <code>{String.raw`\]`}</code> on their own lines for a displayed equation.</p>
        <pre><code>{snippets.display}</code></pre>
      </section>
      <section class="guide-card">
        <h2>A calculation, aligned</h2>
        <p>Use <code>aligned</code>, <code>&amp;</code>, and <code>{String.raw`\\`}</code> to line up steps. Add an explicit number with <code>{String.raw`\tag{1}`}</code>. Automatic numbering, <code>{String.raw`\label`}</code>, and <code>{String.raw`\eqref`}</code> are not supported.</p>
        <pre><code>{snippets.aligned}</code></pre>
      </section>
      <section class="guide-card">
        <h2>Words with structure</h2>
        <p>Headings create the document outline. Lists, links, emphasis, and quotations give your reader a path through the page.</p>
        <pre><code>{snippets.structure}</code></pre>
      </section>
      <section class="guide-card">
        <h2>Keep values together</h2>
        <p>Separate table columns with pipes. Colons control alignment. Escape a pipe within a cell as <code>{String.raw`\|`}</code>, including inside mathematical notation.</p>
        <pre><code>{snippets.table}</code></pre>
      </section>
      <section class="guide-card">
        <h2>Code stays code</h2>
        <p>Wrap inline code in backticks, or use a fenced block with a language name. Mathematical delimiters inside code remain literal. Only an explicit <code>interactive</code> block can become a runnable demo after you choose Run.</p>
        <pre><code>{snippets.code}</code></pre>
      </section>
      <section class="guide-card">
        <h2>Notes and next steps</h2>
        <p>Footnotes hold supporting detail. Mark tasks in the source with <code>[ ]</code> or <code>[x]</code>; preview checkboxes show their state.</p>
        <pre><code>{snippets.notes}</code></pre>
      </section>
      <section class="guide-card">
        <h2>Your own notation</h2>
        <p>Define a macro with <code>{String.raw`\gdef`}</code> before using it later in the document. Definitions belong to this source and are rebuilt on each preview.</p>
        <pre><code>{snippets.macros}</code></pre>
      </section>
    </div>

    <section aria-labelledby="working-with-documents">
      <h2 id="working-with-documents">A few things to know</h2>
      <p>Mathematics is typeset by KaTeX, which supports a large subset of TeX math. A formula that cannot be rendered stays visible with an error; you can correct it without losing the surrounding document. Raw HTML is displayed as text. The explicit <code>interactive</code> syntax runs supported HTML and scripts only inside an isolated frame after you choose Run; MDX is not evaluated.</p>
      <p>Live preview, pasting, and the formatting tools support up to 400,000 characters. Work with smaller documents when a longer text exceeds that limit. Changing a document style leaves its Markdown source untouched.</p>
      <p>Imported pictures are stored separately from the Markdown text, so they do not consume its character allowance. PNG, JPEG, WebP, and GIF data URLs are also supported, but long embedded strings do count toward that limit.</p>
      <p>Drafts save in this browser. Download <strong>Project ZIP</strong> for a complete lesson project, <strong>Markdown</strong> for a text copy, <strong>Markdown + images</strong> for local pictures, or <strong>Slide bundle</strong> to retain a deck’s slide settings. <strong>HTML</strong> keeps a portable document or presentation, and <strong>Save PDF</strong> opens your browser’s print dialog. Clearing site data can remove local drafts, so keep downloaded copies of work you want to retain.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="keyboard-shortcuts">
      <h2 id="keyboard-shortcuts">Stay at the keyboard</h2>
      <p>In the workspace, use <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> on Windows or Linux and <kbd>⌘</kbd> on macOS:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><kbd>Ctrl / ⌘ + S</kbd> — download the current Markdown.</li>
        <li><kbd>Ctrl / ⌘ + O</kbd> — open a Markdown file or an exported ZIP bundle.</li>
        <li><kbd>Ctrl / ⌘ + F</kbd> — find text in the editor.</li>
        <li><kbd>Ctrl / ⌘ + P</kbd> — prepare a document or slide PDF.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>
    <p><a class="button-primary" href={withBase('?example=quickstart')}>Try the field guide <Icon name="arrow" size={16} /></a></p>
    <p>Questions about local drafts, exports, or reuse? Read the <a href={withBase('faq/')}>Markdown and PDF FAQ</a>.</p>
  </main>
  <SiteFooter />
</BaseLayout>
